PR #2599  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Underspecified Svnapot G-stage support may cause the N-bit to be silently ignored in G-stage PTEs, producing 4 KiB mappings where software expects 64 KiB, corrupting guest-physical-to-real-physical address translation.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/pgtable-64.h:79-87  (global PTE format)
      The PTE format defines bit 63 as _PAGE_NAPOT in a shared pte_t used by both VS-stage (host) and G-stage (guest) page tables. There is no per-stage scoping — no separate type or bitmask distinguishes N-bit semantics for G-stage vs VS-stage. The format comment at line 74-78 labels bit 63 as 'N' without specifying which translation stages honor it. This is the root of the ambiguity: any code operating on a pte_t cannot tell whether N-bit is meaningful for the target translation stage.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/pgtable-64.h:172-175  (global)
      _PAGE_CHG_MASK does not include _PAGE_NAPOT, so the N-bit (bit 63) survives pte_modify(). If a G-stage PTE inadvertently acquires the N-bit through any code path, subsequent pte_modify() calls will preserve it rather than scrub it. Currently G-stage PTEs are never constructed with the N-bit set, so this is latent, but the mask provides no defense-in-depth.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/pgtable.h:289-294  has_svnapot
      has_svnapot() queries whether the Svnapot ISA extension is present on the host CPU but is never consulted by G-stage page table construction code (gstage.c, mmu.c). The G-stage code constructs PTEs via pfn_pte() without checking whether the hardware supports NAPOT in G-stage translation. The implicit invariant is that if the host has Svnapot, G-stage also honors it — but the ISA spec historically didn't guarantee this, and implementations may diverge.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/pgtable.h:322-330  pte_pfn
      pte_pfn() consumes the N-bit via has_svnapot()/pte_napot() to extract the correct PFN from NAPOT PTEs. Since host (VS-stage) and guest (G-stage) share pte_t, this function would NAPOT-decode a G-stage PTE that had its N-bit set. Currently G-stage PTEs never have N-bit set, so the branch is never taken for G-stage PTEs, but there is no type-system or runtime guard preventing cross-contamination between stages.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/gstage.c:22-23  gstage_pte_leaf
      gstage_pte_leaf() checks only _PAGE_READ|_PAGE_WRITE|_PAGE_EXEC for leaf detection. It does NOT consider _PAGE_NAPOT. This is correct behavior: a NAPOT PTE is still a leaf PTE (it maps a contiguous physical range rather than pointing to the next page-table level). The N-bit is an attribute on a leaf PTE, not an alternative leaf indicator. No change needed here.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/gstage.c:124-164  kvm_riscv_gstage_set_pte
      At line 158, set_pte() directly writes map->pte into the G-stage page table with no masking of the N-bit (bit 63). The calling function kvm_riscv_gstage_map_page() constructs the PTE via pfn_pte() + pte_mkdirty(), neither of which sets the N-bit, so the current behavior is safe. However, the function performs zero validation that map->pte does not contain _PAGE_NAPOT. There is no defensive masking, no WARN_ON, and no compile-time assertion. The implicit invariant 'G-stage PTEs never carry the N-bit' is not enforced anywhere in this code path.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/mmu.c:448-547  kvm_riscv_mmu_map
      kvm_riscv_mmu_map() is the top-level entry point mapping host pages into the G-stage. It calls kvm_riscv_gstage_map_page() (lines 547-551) which constructs G-stage PTEs from host PFNs. No has_svnapot() check is performed, and no N-bit filtering is applied. The PTE construction in kvm_riscv_gstage_map_page() uses pfn_pte() which does not set the N-bit, so the current behavior is safe. However, this is the bridge point between host (VS-stage) and guest (G-stage) page tables: host huge-page info (PMD_SIZE, PUD_SIZE, transparent_hugepage_adjust) flows into G-stage mapping decisions without any NAPOT-awareness. If a future change propagates host-level NAPOT metadata into G-stage PTEs through this path, there is no guard to catch it.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/pgtable.h:536-540  pte_leaf_size
      pte_leaf_size() uses pte_napot() to determine page size, falling back to PAGE_SIZE for non-NAPOT PTEs. This is a generic MM helper — no G-stage-specific counterpart exists. If called on a G-stage PTE that had its N-bit set (currently impossible but architecturally unguarded), the function would report a 64 KiB NAPOT mapping that the hardware may not actually honor in G-stage, leading to incorrect TLB maintenance or page-fault decisions.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_onereg.c:28-48  kvm_isa_ext_arr
      KVM exposes SVNAPOT as an emulated ISA extension to the guest via KVM_RISCV_ISA_EXT_SVNAPOT (line 48). This tells the guest OS that it can legitimately use NAPOT pages in its VS-stage page tables. The guest's VS-stage use of NAPOT is fine — VS-stage translation is handled by hardware. However, the host's G-stage code has no corresponding NAPOT support: if a guest GPA falls within a NAPOT mapping and the host must emulate or walk the guest's page tables, the host has no G-stage NAPOT-aware walker. More critically, the exposure of Svnapot to the guest combined with G-stage's N-bit ignorance creates an asymmetry: the guest believes NAPOT semantics are available system-wide, but the host's second-stage translation layer is entirely oblivious to the feature.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_onereg.c:190-190  riscv_vcpu_get_isa_ext_single
      SVNAPOT (line 190) is listed among extensions that cannot be disabled by the VMM — there is no architectural config bit to turn it off. This means if Svnapot is present on the host silicon, the guest ALWAYS sees it as available. The guest will thus generate NAPOT PTEs in its VS-stage, which is correct. But the host G-stage code remains unprepared to handle NAPOT semantics if it ever needs to interpret those guest PTEs (e.g., for MMIO emulation, device assignment, or page-fault handling). The invariant 'G-stage never needs to interpret guest PTEs with N-bit set' is implicit and unchecked.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/Kconfig:579-596  (Kconfig entry)
      RISCV_ISA_SVNAPOT Kconfig help text describes Svnapot as 'supervisor mode NAPOT pages' and discusses virtual-to-physical translation only in the context of base page size and hugetlbfs integration. There is no mention of whether Svnapot applies to G-stage (hypervisor-accelerated/second-stage) translation, nor any dependency on KVM or virtualization configs. This documentation ambiguity mirrors the ISA-spec ambiguity that the PR aims to fix: without an explicit statement that Svnapot is or is not usable in G-stage PTEs, kernel developers and hardware implementers lack clear guidance.

