Spec: Underspecified Svnapot G-stage support may cause the N-bit to be silently ignored in G-stage PTEs, producing 4 KiB mappings where software expects 64 KiB, corrupting guest-physical-to-real-physical address translation.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/pte.h:131-132  (global defines)
      PTE_HI_MASK unconditionally masks out bits 63-54 (including the N-bit at bit 63) in all PTE physical-address extraction paths, regardless of translation stage. The comment says these are 'reserved for future use', but Svnapot IS detected by identcpu (SV_SVNAPOT bit). This means bit 63 is treated as reserved/stripped even when Svnapot is present, so NAPOT pages are never actually created. This is accidentally safe for G-stage (the N-bit can never be set in any PTE), but it constitutes a hidden invariant: the code relies on PTE_HI_MASK to suppress the N-bit rather than explicitly scoping Svnapot enablement per translation stage. If PTE_HI_MASK were ever relaxed to allow the N-bit through for VS-stage, G-stage PTEs would also gain the N-bit without a corresponding henvcfg gate, creating the exact violation described in the spec.Wrote 3 report(s) → output/riscv-isa-manual/pr-2599/

  [RISK] sys/riscv/include/riscvreg.h:176-177  (global defines)
      The henvcfg register bit definitions include HENVCFG_STCE (bit 63) and HENVCFG_PBMTE (bit 62) but have no definition for a Svnapot G-stage control bit (e.g., NTPBTE or equivalent). In the ratified RISC-V privileged architecture, henvcfg contains a bit that controls whether Svnapot is honored in G-stage translation. The absence of this definition means the hypervisor has no programmatic means to either explicitly enable or explicitly disable G-stage Svnapot. Combined with identcpu.c detecting SV_SVNAPOT, this creates a specification gap: the code knows Svnapot exists but cannot express whether it applies to G-stage, leaving the decision to hardware-default behavior that may differ across implementations.
  [OK] sys/riscv/vmm/vmm_riscv.c:620-635  vmmops_run
      The hypervisor configures G-stage translation by writing hgatp and conditionally setting HENVCFG_STCE in henvcfg. No Svnapot-enable bit is written to henvcfg, so G-stage Svnapot is never explicitly enabled. Combined with PTE_HI_MASK stripping bit 63, G-stage PTEs will never contain the N-bit. This is safe under the current code: the N-bit cannot appear in G-stage PTEs, so the violation condition (N-bit set in G-stage PTE on an Svnapot-in-VS-only implementation) cannot occur.
  [RISK] sys/riscv/riscv/identcpu.c:173-180  parse_isa_fdt
      The SV_SVNAPOT flag is parsed from the ISA string and stored, but it is treated as a monolithic supervisor-mode capability with no per-stage decomposition. The code at line 177 uses CHECK_S_EXT('svnapot', SV_SVNAPOT) which bundles VS-stage and G-stage Svnapot support into a single boolean. There is no separate detection path for hypervisor-level Svnapot capability (e.g., from the hypervisor ISA string or a separate DT property), and no code that queries whether the implementation honors Svnapot in G-stage specifically. This is the root of the 'ambiguous extension scope' pattern: the flag SV_SVNAPOT is semantically overloaded to mean 'some form of Svnapot exists' without qualifying which translation stages are covered.
  [OK] sys/riscv/riscv/pmap.c:1611-1666  pmap_pinit_stage
      The stage-2 PMAP initialization correctly allocates a separate 4-page root table for G-stage (line 1625-1627) and skips VS-stage-specific kernel L1 entry copying (line 1638). The same PTE construction macros and PTE_HI_MASK are used for both stages, meaning the N-bit is uniformly stripped in G-stage PTEs. This is currently safe: no G-stage NAPOT mappings are created. However, the function has no explicit assertion or check that Svnapot is NOT being used in stage-2 context — it relies entirely on the caller never passing the N-bit in the 'bits' parameter to PTE construction macros.


